{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,25]],"date-time":"2026-09-25T15:04:10Z","timestamp":1790348650319,"version":"4.1.0"},"reference-count":63,"publisher":"Wiley","issue":"3","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,16]],"date-time":"2026-07-16T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1784160000000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"},{"start":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,16]],"date-time":"2026-07-16T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1784160000000},"content-version":"tdm","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http:\/\/doi.wiley.com\/10.1002\/tdm_license_1.1"}],"content-domain":{"domain":["rsnz.onlinelibrary.wiley.com"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"short-container-title":["New Zealand Journal of Marine and Freshwater Research"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,9]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>\n                    Blue carbon ecosystems (BCE), including mangroves, saltmarshes, and seagrasses, provide essential functions and services globally but are increasingly challenged by climate change and other anthropogenic activities. In Aotearoa New Zealand (Aotearoa NZ), there is mounting interest in blue carbon as a nature\u2010based solution to climate change. Quantifying the monetary co\u2010benefits of BCE, in addition to carbon sequestration, can help illustrate the relative contribution of ecosystem services provided by existing habitats, as well as the potential additional value associated with restoration. Here, we estimated the value of provisioning, regulating, and cultural ecosystem services provided by BCE in Aotearoa NZ. We found that the most valuable ecosystem service provided by mangroves was carbon sequestration, while in saltmarsh and seagrass, the most valuable ecosystem services were waste treatment and existence, bequest values, respectively. Unit monetary valuations of ecosystem services range from NZD 3\u2009ha\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    yr\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    (water flow regulation) to NZD 32,590\u2009ha\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    yr\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    (climate regulation) for mangroves, NZD 8\u2009ha\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    yr\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    (spiritual experience) to NZD 23,045\u2009ha\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    yr\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    (waste treatment) for saltmarshes, and NZD 3\u2009ha\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    yr\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    (water regulation) to NZD 14,512\u2009ha\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    yr\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    (existence, bequest values) for seagrasses. The estimated national potential monetary value of ecosystem services provided by current habitats ranges from NZD 0.8\u2013995\u2009 million yr\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    for mangroves (30,533\u2009ha nationally), NZD 0.2\u2013482\u2009million yr\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    for saltmarshes (20,932\u2009ha nationally), and NZD 0.2\u2013216\u2009million yr\n                    <jats:sup>\u22121<\/jats:sup>\n                    for seagrasses (61,340\u2009ha nationally). If drained areas were restored from drained agricultural use, values would increase accordingly. These results apply the benefit transfer approach (applying monetary values from analogous temperate ecosystems globally to estimate values in Aotearoa NZ) to generate indicative monetary benchmarks, which can help inform discussions around investment in BCE and long\u2010term coastal climate change adaptation decision\u2010making in Aotearoa NZ.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1002\/nzm2.70056","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,16]],"date-time":"2026-07-16T17:25:11Z","timestamp":1784222711000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/crossmark_policy","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Beyond Blue Carbon: Estimating the Value of Ecosystem Services Provided by Coastal Wetlands in Aotearoa New Zealand"],"prefix":"10.1002","volume":"60","author":[{"given":"Phoebe","family":"Stewart\u2010Sinclair","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Earth Sciences New Zealand  Hamilton New Zealand"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2238-5098","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zoe","family":"Qu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Earth Sciences New Zealand  Hamilton New Zealand"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6805-6260","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Orlando","family":"Lam\u2010Gordillo","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Earth Sciences New Zealand  Hamilton New Zealand"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9412-827X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Richard","family":"Bulmer","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Tidal Research  Auckland New Zealand"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1076-3995","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Vonda","family":"Cummings","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Earth Sciences New Zealand  Hamilton New Zealand"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-2101-7237","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Carolyn","family":"Lundquist","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Earth Sciences New Zealand  Hamilton New Zealand"},{"name":"School of Environment University of Auckland  Auckland New Zealand"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"311","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,16]]},"reference":[{"key":"e_1_2_7_2_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1111\/rec.14241"},{"key":"e_1_2_7_3_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1111\/j.1365-2427.2010.02412.x"},{"key":"e_1_2_7_4_1","unstructured":"Australia.2023. Nature Repair Market (Consequential Amendments) Bill 2023."},{"key":"e_1_2_7_5_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/B978-0-444-63893-9.00027-7"},{"key":"e_1_2_7_6_1","volume-title":"The Carbon Challenge: New Zealand's Emissions Trading Scheme","author":"Bertram G.","year":"2021"},{"key":"e_1_2_7_7_1","volume-title":"Communities and Nature Markets: Building Just Partnerships in Biodiversity Credits","author":"Biodiversity Credit Alliance","year":"2023"},{"key":"e_1_2_7_8_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1002\/aqc.2442"},{"key":"e_1_2_7_9_1","volume-title":"California Salt Marsh Accretion, Ecosystem Services, and Disturbance Responses in the Face of Climate Change","author":"Brown L. N.","year":"2019"},{"key":"e_1_2_7_10_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1111\/rec.14225"},{"key":"e_1_2_7_11_1","unstructured":"Bulmer R. P.Stewart\u2010Sinclair O.Lam\u2010Gordillo andG.Flowers.2024. \u201cBlue Carbon Potential in the Otago Region.\u201d Prepared for Otago Regional Council. Auckland New Zealand Tidal Research.28."},{"key":"e_1_2_7_12_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1002\/ecy.2045"},{"key":"e_1_2_7_13_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016\/j.ecolecon.2011.11.011"},{"key":"e_1_2_7_14_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1088\/1748-9326\/ad7044"},{"key":"e_1_2_7_15_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1080\/14486563.2024.2425652"},{"key":"e_1_2_7_16_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1071\/MF17377"},{"key":"e_1_2_7_17